[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SVG (Scalable Vector Graphics): Embedding and Manipulating Vector Graphics in HTML5"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) is an XML-based vector image format for two-dimensional graphics with support for interactivity and animation. Unlike raster image formats like JPEG or PNG, SVG images are defined using mathematical equations, making them scalable without losing quality. HTML5 provides several ways to embed and manipulate SVG images within web pages."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Embedding SVG in HTML5"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are three primary methods for embedding SVG into HTML5:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inline SVG: Embedding the SVG code directly within the HTML document."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Object Tag: Using the `<object>` tag to reference an external SVG file."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Img Tag: Using the `<img>` tag to reference an external SVG file (with limitations)."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Inline SVG"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Embedding SVG code directly into your HTML document offers the most flexibility for manipulation with CSS and JavaScript.  It also avoids extra HTTP requests."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"<svg width="100" height="100">
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" stroke="green" stroke-width="4" fill="yellow" />
</svg>"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code creates a simple yellow circle with a green border. The `width` and `height` attributes define the SVG canvas size. `cx` and `cy` define the center coordinates of the circle, and `r` defines the radius."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Object Tag"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `<object>` tag allows you to embed an external SVG file. This is useful for reusing the same SVG image across multiple pages."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"<object data="circle.svg" type="image/svg+xml" width="100" height="100"></object>"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `data` specifies the path to the SVG file, and `type` specifies the MIME type.  `width` and `height` define the dimensions of the embedded SVG."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a file named `circle.svg` with the following content:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"<svg width="100" height="100" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" stroke="green" stroke-width="4" fill="yellow" />
</svg>"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Img Tag"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `<img>` tag can also be used to embed SVG files. However, this method has limitations. You cannot directly manipulate the SVG content with CSS or JavaScript from the parent HTML document.  It's treated as a single image."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"<img src="circle.svg" alt="Circle" width="100" height="100">"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"This is the simplest way to embed an SVG, but it offers the least control."},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Manipulating SVG with CSS"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using inline SVG, you can style SVG elements directly with CSS, just like any other HTML element.  You can use inline styles, internal stylesheets (within the `<style>` tag), or external stylesheets."},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"<svg width="100" height="100">
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" stroke="green" stroke-width="4" fill="yellow" class="my-circle" />
</svg>

<style>
  .my-circle {
    fill: red;
    stroke: blue;
    stroke-width: 8;
  }
</style>"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the circle's fill color is changed to red, the stroke color to blue, and the stroke width to 8 using CSS."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Manipulating SVG with JavaScript"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"JavaScript allows you to dynamically modify SVG attributes and properties, enabling interactive and animated graphics. You can select SVG elements using standard DOM methods like `getElementById` or `querySelector`."},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"<svg width="100" height="100">
  <circle id="myCircle" cx="50" cy="50" r="40" stroke="green" stroke-width="4" fill="yellow" />
</svg>

<script>
  const circle = document.getElementById('myCircle');
  circle.addEventListener('click', function() {
    circle.setAttribute('fill', 'purple');
    circle.setAttribute('r', '50');
  });
</script>"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"This code adds a click event listener to the circle. When the circle is clicked, its fill color changes to purple, and its radius increases to 50."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Animation with SVG"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"SVG provides built-in animation capabilities using elements like `<animate>`, `<animateTransform>`, and `<animateMotion>`. These elements allow you to change attributes over time."},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"<svg width="200" height="200">
  <rect x="0" y="0" width="50" height="50" fill="red">
    <animate attributeName="x" from="0" to="150" dur="3s" repeatCount="indefinite" />
  </rect>
</svg>"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example animates the `x` attribute of a rectangle, moving it from 0 to 150 over 3 seconds. The `repeatCount="indefinite"` attribute makes the animation loop continuously."},{"order":34,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"SVG offers a powerful way to create scalable and interactive graphics for the web. By understanding the different embedding methods and manipulation techniques using CSS and JavaScript, you can leverage SVG to enhance your web applications with visually appealing and dynamic content."}]
